<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The scene life cycle, which iOS 26 and later require.

	  Without a manifest UIKit kills the app on launch rather than warning:

	      EXC_BREAKPOINT (SIGTRAP) in
	      ___UIApplicationEvaluateRuntimeIssueForNoSceneLifecycleAdoption_block_

	  Tauri's iOS template does not add this file, and the CLI merges it over the
	  generated `Info.plist` **at build time** — `tauri ios init` alone does not —
	  so the built app's plist is where to check it landed.

	  `UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes` is `true` for the same reason Hanzi
	  Tutor's is: that is what has launched on the test device (HHIP1, iOS 27)
	  through `tao` 0.36+ and `tauri` 2.12. It declares multi-window support, which
	  is not what UIKit actually requires here — `tao` now decides by the mere
	  *presence* of this manifest (`platform_impl/ios/scene.rs`) — and this app is
	  single-window, so `false` is the more honest declaration. It is not worth a
	  second variable on a first launch: changing it changes windowing behaviour
	  and wants its own tested change. See `HANDOVER.md` §6 for the long version.

	  There is deliberately no microphone string. This app never records: it speaks
	  through the system synthesiser and `hanzi-voice` is built without its capture
	  feature, so `cpal` is not linked at all.
	-->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<!--
	  The model check's *local* half (N24, HANDOVER_NIHONGO.md invariant 43).

	  App Transport Security refuses cleartext HTTP by default, and the endpoint a
	  learner is most likely to point this at on a phone is a language model running
	  on their own computer — `http://192.168.x.x:11434/v1` and the like. Without
	  these two keys the request cannot leave the app at all: the cloud half (HTTPS)
	  works either way, and the local half would fail with an ATS error that reads
	  like a broken feature rather than a policy.

	  The exception is deliberately the **narrow** one. `NSAllowsLocalNetworking`
	  permits plain HTTP to local hosts — unqualified names, `.local` and the private
	  address ranges — while leaving ATS in force for everything else, so an
	  internet endpoint is still required to be HTTPS. `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` would
	  have been the blunt version, and it is not here.

	  `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription` is the string iOS 14 and later shows in the
	  one-time "allow this app to find devices on your local network" prompt. It is
	  worded for this app's single use of the local network, and nothing is scanned
	  or discovered: the app connects to the one address the learner typed, and only
	  on the button press.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Nihongo Tutor connects to the model server you configure — for example a language model running on your own computer — to check a sentence you have written. Nothing is sent unless you press the button, and no device is scanned or discovered.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
